Tests for the CIVS web pages
============================

These cover the client-side half of CIVS: the markup and JavaScript that
install-civs generates from html/*.base. They run under Node.js using
jsdom, so no web server, no CGI, and no installed copy of CIVS is needed.
The pages under test are built into tests/work, which is disposable.

    tests/run-tests               run everything
    tests/run-tests questions     run one suite (matched as a substring)

Node.js must be installed. On the first run, jsdom is fetched into
tests/node_modules; neither directory is part of a release.

The suites
----------

substitution  Builds html/create_poll.html.base in all the languages
              install-civs generates, and checks that no @Token@ is left
              unresolved. This catches a malformed entry in
              html/static-translations.txt, which otherwise silently
              swallows every entry after it. The @PLACEHOLDER@ settings
              that install-civs rather than subst_text.pl fills in are
              allowed to remain, except MAX_QUESTIONS: that one reaches
              the page as a JavaScript number, so the runner substitutes
              it, using a value other than the shipped default so that the
              tests notice if it stops arriving.

questions     The multi-question poll creation UI: adding, removing and
              reordering questions, the field names and question indices
              that result, header numbering, label and aria associations,
              and form validation.

shadowing     Checks, in every language, that no function called from an
              inline event handler shares a name with a form control's id
              or name. Such a name is shadowed, because inline handlers
              resolve names against the enclosing form before the global
              scope, and a form finds its controls by id as well as by
              name. The symptom is "x is not a function" on click. This
              has to be checked statically: jsdom does not implement the
              form named getter, so clicking things will not reproduce it.

nojs          Checks that the page degrades correctly with scripting
              disabled -- the multi-question controls stay hidden and the
              form posts the same fields a single-question poll always
              has.

Adding a suite
--------------

Write tests/test-<name>.js taking the page to test as its first argument,
exiting non-zero on failure, then add a block for it in run-tests. The
existing suites share no harness; each is a standalone script of about a
hundred lines, which has so far been easier to read than a framework.
